The function f is differentiable on the closed interval [0, 6], and f(1) = 4. The graph of f′, the derivative of f, consists of three line segments:
from (0, 1) to (2, 5), a segment of slope 2;
from (2, 5) to (5, −1), a segment of slope −2;
from (5, −1) to (6, −1), a horizontal segment.
The graph of f′ crosses the x-axis at x = 4.5.

A particle moves along the x-axis. Its velocity v, in meters per second, is continuous for 0 ≤ t ≤ 10 seconds, and its graph consists of four line segments:
• from (0, 0) to (2, 4);
• from (2, 4) to (6, −4), crossing the t-axis at t = 4;
• from (6, −4) to (8, −4);
• from (8, −4) to (10, 0).
At time t = 0 the particle is at position x = 5. No calculator is allowed.
